here's the truth living on the road  these past nine months  has been weird the overall vibe is just  really hectic  and basically like kind of the opposite  of why i got into this lifestyle  [Music]  uh  [Applause]  hey guys what's up so uh thank you for  being here  if you are new my name is christian  shafer i'm an outdoor adventure  photographer  and i live in my van full time i've been  living on the road for two and a half  years  and yeah so the last time that we caught  up um  sorry there's a truck going by but the  last time that we caught up i was in  arizona  and i am back in arizona now but between  the last video in this video  a few things have happened i did some  traveling um  and i wanted to update you guys on that  and then also  just kind of like some issues i've been  running into  with living on the road during the  second wave of the pandemic like  i just kind of want to give you guys  more of a  big picture overview like show some  different sides of what it what  this experience has been like because  it's definitely been challenging lately  so yeah um like i said thanks for being  here and i'll just dive right in  so i was in arizona and the weather down  here is perfect  i was camped out in the wilderness and  it was really great  but i had a house sit coming up a pet  sit coming up  that i had scheduled back in um november  and i couldn't exactly cancel it nor  did i want to because i would have left  the the pet owners kind of like high and  dry  so um i had this upcoming pet sit in san  francisco  and things were kind of escalating in  california and it was looking like maybe  things would shut down again  so i was a little bit nervous about  driving all the way out there but like i  said what could i do  so drove out to california and literally  the day  after i did uh california went into  lockdown and they had the  stay-at-home order so that was  unfortunate  um for road life and because the plan  was to stay  was to drive out to san francisco do the  two-week house set  and then stay on california like blm  land travel along the coast and do some  low-key  slow travel um and just still be able to  shower at the campgrounds and the gyms  but with the lockdown all of that was  closed i tried not to think about that  too much  and went ahead with the pet sit which  was amazing  it was in mill valley so it was just  beautiful like the house was amazing and  the two  puppies were so cute oh my gosh there  were two french little bulldogs  and i did the house set with nathaniel  um he's also an outdoor adventure  photographer so it works out really well  for us to do the pet sitting together  because if one of us gets a photo  assignment or we need to go travel  the other person can stay and take care  of the pets so we were there together  and we got out on some adventures we  went um down to the beach which was near  the house we went out to mount tam  and honestly everything was on lockdown  and there was a curfew but  things from the outside i mean they seem  pretty normal still  like we could go to the grocery store we  could go shopping  there was just limited capacity at the  stores we could still  take the dogs out for a walk and go for  a hike as long as we were wearing a mask  and being socially distanced  so yeah it was actually like a really  nice enjoyable time where we could rest  and relax and just kind of like enjoy  being off the road  but when that ended we decided to go up  to point reyes  and hang out there and see how feasible  it was to road life  and oh my gosh it's so beautiful up  there  [Music]  huh  there were some great places to camp and  very easy to social isolate  um but you know by day four i was like  i'd really love a shower  i've been showered since saturday and  it's now thursday so  awesome this is becoming the new norm  with this coronavirus lockdown our only  option  to shower was to go to a truck stop rent  a hotel  or get an airbnb and thought about  just doing truck stops until the  lockdown ended but  the nearest truck stop was like 30  minutes out of the way which seemed  not really very practical we were  talking about it and we thought okay  why don't we just rent an airbnb for a  month  and we can split the cost and that way  we can do it in a cool location  so we can go do like three to four  nights out in the backcountry and we can  take turns even if we want and go do  photo shoots and stuff like that  and just have a place to come back and  shower and have wi-fi and all of that  so that's what we did we booked a  one-month airbnb  and to be honest like it kind of felt  like i was giving up  like i know that sounds stupid but i  almost feel like i'm waving the white  flag when i get a hotel or an airbnb  nate went off and did his own thing and  i did my own thing in socal and then we  met up  and this airbnb that we had booked was  just like  it was a great location it was near  alabama hills death valley and a lot of  other really cool blm locations where we  could go shoot  but the airbnb was just not what we  imagined it would be  like i won't go into too much detail  i'll just say that like  it was super loud it was on an  intersection the walls were really thin  i had a conference call and i had to  lock myself in the bathroom and even  then you could still hear the cars  just roaring by so and there were a few  other things  but like i said i won't go into detail  but  the most important thing is like the guy  who ran the airbnb was so cool he was  great at communication and when we just  told him like hey this probably isn't  going to work for us  we do a lot of audio based work he was  like no problem  he let us cancel and just pay for the  days that we were there which he totally  didn't have to do so  thank you uh that was really nice of him  anyway so we found ourselves back out on  the road  at this point we're like okay we  have a couple options we can either find  another airbnb  um we can cross back into arizona in the  land of  showers or we can maybe go find some blm  land in california  and live off of truck stops and solar  shower  i decided to come back to arizona i'll  admit i have some mixed feelings  about coming back here especially with  everything going on it's a hot spot at  the moment  and there's a lot of unknowns but at the  end of the day  um i don't have a ton of options  aside from like i said renting another  airbnb or buying a house getting a hotel  you can easily social distance out here  even when i went to the gym which i've  been twice now  it's completely empty and i wear a mask  but it's been interesting trying to  navigate you know like weighing  those things like should i go should i  stay because here's the truth  living on the road these past nine  months has been weird  like i won't say that it's been hard  um i know a lot of you out there are  going through way way worse  i'm not trying to complain i just want  to paint a fuller picture for you guys  of  what this has been like because it's  maybe sometimes it looks like it's just  carefree and beautiful and fun and a lot  of times it is  but there's also some downsides so i  just want to share some of those with  you guys to paint a fuller picture  aside from gyms being closed and not  having a solid shower option  except for here in arizona where they're  still open um campgrounds are also  closed  uh or if they're not they're they're  like jam-packed and i get it everyone  wants to get outside  they want to leave their house they want  to go recreate and like only so many  places are open  so naturally they're going to be  overcrowded but a consequence of that is  just that  these places that are usually low-key  and relatively crowd-free  are just overrun with people and trash  is overflowing and in some cases  like there's graffiti and things are  being destroyed  and that's kind of sad to see the  overall vibe is just really hectic  and basically like kind of the opposite  of why i got into this lifestyle one  thing i love about being on the road is  being able to distance yourself from  people like i'm a natural social  distancer i'm also an introvert so  yeah it's hard to do that in these  places that are still open and just like  overrun with crowds  with the campground closures there are a  few other things that are impacting this  lifestyle and that's like having places  to fill the water jugs having places to  dump my toilet and my gray water tank  i usually do that at campgrounds and and  also the showers  and sometimes what that means is i have  to drive way out of my way to go  dump my grey water and toilet which  whatever that's what you have to do to  live this lifestyle  but it does mean unnecessary travel  which is kind of the opposite  of what we'd want to be doing during a  pandemic  it's also just a massive waste of time  gas and money  libraries and coffee shops are closed or  at least indoor seating  is not available and that's kind of been  a big adjustment for me because i know i  have the van to work in  and that's amazing and i love that but  i'm super adhd  and sometimes i literally need to just  walk into the library  and reserve one of those study rooms and  lock myself in there in order to get  real work done like i'm so easily  distracted it's kind of funny  i do get comments fairly often saying  there's no way you actually live in that  fan it's way too clean like someone who  actually lives in that van  it would be a mess and thanks  i guess but like there are a couple  reasons why that is the case  first of all i'm a neat freak but second  of all  i need like very minimal surroundings  very zen surroundings because otherwise  i'm just i'm like a squirrel i'm like  so distracted and it's also an avoidance  tactic  if i'm sitting down to do hard work and  i just don't feel like working  i'll take a look around and be like oh i  could organize that drawer  or i should really clean this wooden  panel even though it doesn't need it  these things on top of the usual  struggles around like getting mail  receiving product shipments for work um  juggling photo assignments and  print sales and emails and keeping track  of expenses for taxes  and just like everything related to  running a business  it's all magnified when you live on the  road because  just like simple tasks like for example  intercepting  a a package for a product i need to  shoot to create content around  which should be simple right just comes  to your house that can turn into like a  full day ordeal of going back and forth  on email  and changing the address and all of  these things  so yeah and then like finding places to  do laundry  places to sleep at night um dealing with  general van maintenance like  oil changes and you know like fixing  things here and there  with the build maintaining that um and  then also maintaining some form of  community and like  staying in touch with family those  things are a little more difficult  for everyone um considering that we're  supposed to keep our distance but  being on the road you already feel a  little bit isolated  and the pandemic has really kind of like  magnified that  so that's just a little update on what  i've been up to  and what it's been like living on the  road lately um  mostly the crappy stuff but i just want  to be honest with  you guys when it comes to that um  the truth though is that like despite  all of these challenges i still love it  out here  like i love sitting by the campfire and  seeing stars at night before i fall  asleep i love  waking up with the sunrise i love the  freedom and the feeling of not being  tethered to any one place  for now i will say that this past year  has taught me that i very much want to  buy land and build my own place and yeah  i'd love to do that in the next year or  two  um at this point i still don't know  which state i want to buy it in and  that's kind of what i'm trying to figure  out by all this  moving around i've only narrowed it down  to the west  west of the rockies but having a place  to call home  and hunker down during times like this  and then also  have the van to go out and travel that's  like my dream  so hopefully in the next year or two  it's my goal to put out more videos  moving forward  so thanks for sticking around head over  to instagram and follow me there  and yeah see you next time  you

